From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 11:57:13 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10775/11171] [outer-loop] Env-gated --parallel launch flag for
 the single-stream induce path (REQ-ARC-WMTE-6870)

Measured 2026-09-02: with no --parallel, llama-server auto-picks 4
kv_unified slots sharing one -c pool. A single fresh stream owns the
whole pool (a 15000-token ignore_eos generation completed in full at
-c 49152, past the n_ctx/4=12288 a divided pool would impose). The
offline eval runs games sequentially, so it never needs the 4 slots.

CARNOT_ARC_LLAMA_SERVER_PARALLEL (unset -> flag omitted, 4-slot default,
scored Kaggle path byte-identical; =1 -> one slot, n_ctx_seq=n_ctx, the
lone stream is guaranteed the whole pool). Recorded on the instance as
last_parallel_launch.

SCOPE (honest): this is a SECONDARY improvement, not the whole truncation
fix. The dominant cause of the r11l truncations (18431/2996/4066 of a
26800 budget) is the induce PROMPT SIZE -- it renders every transition
since level-start (k=None), 20431 tokens at 82 transitions and far more
at the 220-299 the live inductions carry -- filling the 49152 pool and
hitting the ctx wall. --parallel 1 guarantees the whole pool but a 30k
prompt on one slot still caps generation near 19k. Remaining levers:
raise -c (VRAM-bounded on 24GB) and/or cap transitions k (accuracy A/B).

The test file (tests/python/test_arc_llama_server_parallel_20260902.py,
11 tests, argv-append mutation proven) landed early via a concurrent
conductor checkpoint's git add -A; this commit adds the code it exercises.

